# Frontend Implementation Plan: Café-Styled Inventory Dashboard

This document details the plan for building a highly aesthetic, Framer-inspired frontend for the Hybrid Inventory Management System. The design is based on the provided "alt portfolio" inspiration, creating a warm, organic, relaxed interface for rigorous supply chain metrics.

## Goal Description

Build a bespoke, Vanilla HTML/CSS/JS single-page application that connects to the existing Flask backend (`/api/products` and `/api/optimize`).
The UI will diverge from typical boring dashboards, adopting a "café-shaped" warm aesthetic featuring:
- **Color Palette:** Off-white/eggshell (`#fffcf5`), deep burgundy (`#511f20`), beige taupe (`#e5dece`), and soft dark coffee (`#785449`).
- **Typography:** Elegant serif headings (Fraunces/Lora) paired with clean sans-serif body text (Hanken Grotesk/Inter) fetched from Google Fonts.
- **Glassmorphism/Soft Shadows:** Smooth, soft-edged elevated card designs to present metrics and forms gracefully.
- **Music Player Hook:** A beautifully styled placeholder music player component positioned neatly in the layout, ready for you to inject your custom song later.
- **Dynamic Charts:** Integration of `Chart.js` to render the 30-day Demand Forecast and the PSO convergence graph seamlessly within this relaxed UI.

## User Review Required

> [!CAUTION]
> **No API Key is Needed at this Stage!** I will use open source CDNs (Chart.js and Google Fonts).
>
> However, for the **Music Player**, I plan to build a sleek embedded interface with a standard `<audio>` tag that you can easily swap the source out for your MP3 later. If you prefer a Spotify/SoundCloud iframe embed placeholder instead, please let me know.

## Proposed Changes

### Frontend Application Layer
---

#### [MODIFY] [index.html](file:///c:/Users/ASUS/Desktop/Inventory-Management-Project/frontend/index.html)
- Create a responsive grid/flex layout with a delicate header.
- Add an optimization form taking `lead_time_days`, `holding_cost`, `ordering_cost`, and `stockout_cost`, with a stylish dropdown for the target `product_id`.
- Build the "Results" section showing numeric cards (`reorder_point`, `optimal_order_qty`, `accuracy`, `decision`).
- Create canvas wrappers for the Forecast and Convergence charts.
- Build the persistent, elegant Music Player component anchored to the screen.

#### [MODIFY] [style.css](file:///c:/Users/ASUS/Desktop/Inventory-Management-Project/frontend/style.css)
- Implement CSS variables for the exact Framer design tokens (colors, font families, shadows, border-radii).
- Add modern micro-animations (e.g., hover lifts, smooth color transitions, soft pulses).
- Design completely custom, non-default input fields, range sliders, or select dropdowns to preserve the premium aesthetic.
- Add responsive media queries to ensure the dashboard looks stunning on mobile devices too.

#### [MODIFY] [main.js](file:///c:/Users/ASUS/Desktop/Inventory-Management-Project/frontend/main.js)
- Fetch `/api/products` on initial load and populate the product dropdown dynamically.
- Handle the `/api/optimize` form submission asynchronously, showing an elegant loading state.
- Mount and update `Chart.js` instances for:
  - **Demand Forecast Chart:** A curved, visually appealing line chart rendering the 30-day predictions.
  - **PSO Optimization Chart:** A graph showing the fitness decreasing over 100 iterations.
- Handle state and update the DOM for KPI text values based on backend responses.

## Open Questions

> [!IMPORTANT]
> 1. Do you want the Flask backend to host these static files, or will you just be running the frontend purely via Live Server or opening `index.html`?
> 2. Is there a default value you prefer for holding cost, ordering cost, lead time, and stockout cost to pre-fill the form?

## Verification Plan

### Automated/Local Tests
- I will verify the code functions smoothly directly within the browser context and handles empty/loading states gracefully.
- Send mock payloads to verify the DOM parsing and chart rendering behaves as expected visually without breaking the layout.

### Manual Verification
- You can run your Flask backend, open the HTML file, perform an optimization run, and observe the beautiful animations and precise Chart.js rendering on your screen.
